ly well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership skills.\n\nWe need to evaluate criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's assess each:\n\nA: Anticipates dangerous situations (good decision quality), sees complex connections (technical competence), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation), but not good at taking criticism (adaptability), unorganized (reliability). Mixed.\n\nB: Very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), but grumpy and uncooperative (crew cooperation, communication). So strengths but cooperation concerns.\n\nC: Can make correct decisions quickly (decision quality), 100% reliable (reliability), creates positive atmosphere (crew cooperation), but difficulty communicating ideas (communication), egocentric (crew cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). Mixed.\n\nD: Responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical competence), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but arrogant (crew cooperation), weak leadership (crew cooperation). So strengths but cooperation concerns.\n\nWe need best overall fit. Which candidate has strongest overall with fewer serious concerns? C has reliability, decision quality, positive crew atmosphere, but communication difficulty and egocentric may be serious for cockpit. B has stress resilience and conscientiousness, but uncooperative and grumpy could be problematic. D has problem solving and responsibility, but arrogance and weak leadership may affect crew. A has technical strengths but unorganized and poor at criticism.\n\nProbably C seems strong on reliability and decision quality, and positive crew atmosph